MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 80a1f2cfa0ad20647bab6ee8a4a9b4f8, type: 3}
  m_Name: FinalShortVirusLecture
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  slides:
  - title: Welcome
    content: Welcome To The Biology Lecture
    slideImage: {fileID: 0}
  - title: SLide1:Openning
    content: Hello everyone! Today, we will look at how the immune system detects,
      responds to, and remembers threats.
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 6558a0ecc8ec5ec47884211ba066a44e, type: 3}
  - title: Slide2:Mosquito
    content: "A mosquito lands on your arm, injects its chemicals into your skin,
      and begins to feed.\r\n You would not even know it was there, if not for the
      red lump that appears, accompanied by a telltale itch.\r\nIt is a nuisance,
      but that bump is also an important signal.\r\n It shows that you are protected
      by your immune system, your body\u2019s major safeguard against infection,
      illness, and disease."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f70914385122c544bacf46d54909df9e, type: 3}
  - title: Slides3:Threats
    content: "This system is a vast network of cells, tissues, and organs that coordinate
      your body\u2019s defenses against any threats to your health.\r\nWithout it,
      you would be exposed to billions of bacteria, viruses, and toxins that could
      make something as minor as a paper cut or a seasonal cold fatal.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 318c6d3b32e5b6d4c9f15bda17a7fb9d, type: 3}
  - title: Slide4:Immune System
    content: "The immune system relies on millions of defensive white blood cells,
      also known as leukocytes, that originate in our bone marrow.\r\n These cells
      migrate into the bloodstream and the lymphatic system, a network of vessels
      that helps clear bodily toxins and waste.\r\nOur bodies are teeming with leukocytes.\r\n
      There are between 4,000 and 11,000 in every microliter of blood."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c6d68dd52bb51104bade09948004c498, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide5: Antigens'
    content: "As they move around, leukocytes work like security personnel, constantly
      screening the blood, tissues, and organs for suspicious signs.\r\n This system
      mainly relies on cues called antigens.\r\n Antigens are molecular traces found
      on the surface of pathogens and other foreign substances, and they betray the
      presence of invaders."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 0d81d1d2b9ba24749a61a580e8ee0488, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide6: Detect Signs'
    content: "As soon as leukocytes detect these signs, it takes only minutes for
      the body\u2019s protective immune response to begin.\r\nThreats to our bodies
      are hugely variable, so the immune response has to be equally adaptable.\r\n
      That means relying on many different types of leukocytes to tackle threats
      in different ways."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: e841b9a6e1f2ed544b327bb088d09a47, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide7: Leukocytes'
    content: "Despite this diversity, leukocytes can be classified into two main
      cellular groups, and these groups coordinate a two-pronged attack.\r\nFirst,
      phagocytes help trigger the immune response by sending macrophages and dendritic
      cells into the blood.\r"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 70aefb1bd7560a3428242fe79a96b02c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide8: Phagocytes'
    content: " As these cells circulate, they destroy any foreign cells they encounter
      simply by consuming them.\r\nThat process also allows phagocytes to identify
      the antigens on the invaders they have just ingested.\r\n They then transmit
      this information to the second major cell group that orchestrates the defense:
      the lymphocytes."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 7a98a591490ec4d488394c8e4dc03d6c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide9: Lymphocytes'
    content: 'A group of lymphocytes called T cells goes in search of infected body
      cells and swiftly kills them off.

      Meanwhile, B cells and helper T cells
      use the information gathered from those unique antigens to begin producing
      special proteins called antibodies.

'
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f87eb916c1996f045b3a1c09f257cbd5, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide10: Antibody'
    content: "This is the pi\xE8ce de r\xE9sistance, the key step in the response.\r\n
      Each antigen has a unique matching antibody that can latch onto it like a lock
      and key and help destroy the invading cells.\r\nB cells can produce millions
      of these antibodies.\r\nThey then cycle through the body and attack the invaders
      until the worst of the threat has been neutralized."
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: d9a348f8a480c084caf7651e63bd1820, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide11: Symptoms'
    content: 'While all of this is going on, familiar symptoms, such as high temperatures
      and swelling, are actually processes designed to aid the immune response.

      A
      warmer body makes it harder for bacteria and viruses to reproduce and spread
      because many of them are temperature-sensitive.

      And when body cells
      are damaged, they release chemicals that make fluid leak into the surrounding
      tissues, causing swelling.

      That swelling also attracts phagocytes,
      which then consume both the invaders and the damaged cells.

      Usually,
      an immune response will eradicate a threat within a few days.

      It will
      not always stop you from getting ill, but that is not its only purpose.

      Its
      actual job is to stop a threat from escalating to dangerous levels inside your
      body.

'
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: b0f7d0c05a5a2054fbb387879e147f4c, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide12: B-T-Cells'
    content: "And through constant surveillance over time, the immune system provides
      another major benefit: it helps us develop long-term immunity.\r\nWhen B cells
      and T cells identify antigens, they can use that information to recognize the
      same invaders in the future.\r\n So when a threat revisits, those cells can
      swiftly deploy the right antibodies to tackle it before it affects many more
      cells.\r\nThat is how you can develop immunity to certain diseases, like chickenpox.\r\n"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 6b28fdccf1d165a45a1d7333f6c9e7fe, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide13: Healthy Cells'
    content: "It does not always work perfectly.\r\nSome people have autoimmune diseases,
      which trick the immune system into attacking the body\u2019s own perfectly
      healthy cells.\r\n No one knows exactly what causes these conditions, but they
      sabotage the immune system to varying degrees and underlie disorders such as
      arthritis, type 1 diabetes, and multiple sclerosis.\r\nFor most individuals,
      however, a healthy immune system will successfully fight off an estimated 300
      colds and innumerable other potential infections over the course of a lifetime.\r\nWithout
      it, those threats would escalate into something far more dangerous.\r\n"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f155c8220c26e354d89771a866ccc647, type: 3}
  - title: 'Slide14: Conclusion'
    content: "So the next time you catch a cold or scratch a mosquito bite, think
      of the immune system.\r\n It is working constantly to protect you.\r\nWe owe
      it our lives.\r\n"
    slideImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 3db5aa5ce6b74284bb199ec9f328a776, type: 3}
